", "Paxton is a polarizing nominee who ousted Cornyn in the primary, putting a usually safe Republican seat in play", "Most raters call it a tossup while markets price Talarico at 64-65 percent, and a Libertarian adds uncertainty"], "rationale": "Polling, fundamentals, and money all point the same way: a narrow Talarico lead of about two points in a state that normally favors Republicans by six, against a damaged Republican nominee. The tossup consensus among most raters and late Republican outside money keep this short of likely Democratic.", "watch": "A late surge of Musk-funded Republican spending or a turnout gap between the two coalitions during early voting could erase the Democrats' two-point edge.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: John Cornyn (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Texas's Senate seat is open for the first time in a generation after John Cornyn lost renomination, and the contest between Democrat James Talarico and Republican Ken Paxton has settled into a narrow Democratic edge. Polls since late September show Talarico ahead by one to six points, averaging about a two- to three-point lead in a state that normally favors Republicans by six.\n\nTalarico has raised roughly $129 million to Paxton's $19 million, and outside groups have spent heavily against Paxton, though Republican-aligned money, including Musk-funded efforts, is arriving late. Most race raters call this a tossup, while prediction markets give Talarico about a 64 to 65 percent chance. A Libertarian candidate adds a wildcard in a state where third-party votes can matter. The race remains genuinely competitive, with the Democrat holding a small but consistent advantage.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6697, "margin": 2.08}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6924, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7255, "margin": 2.8}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6997, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6523, "margin": 2.5}, {"dat